@meta
id: 06-11
title: Drill — t into the closing brace
chapter: 06
type: drill
skills: find-t, line-start
par: 76
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: four careful clicks into four identical gaps, eight times
open: order.events.ts

@brief
Four event payloads, all of them gaining a `reason` field. Each becomes
`{ orderId, reason }`. Eight passes over the file.

The rep is `^` to the start of the code, `t}` to stop just before the
closing brace, `i, reason` `Esc`, `j` down.

@keys
| `^` | first non-blank |
| `t}` | forward until just before the `}` |
| `i` | insert before the cursor |

@hints
If a line comes out as `{ orderId , reason}` you used `f}` instead of `t}`.
Press `u` and do that line again.
--
Ignore the event names entirely. Identical fingerwork is the whole exercise;
the file is only there to make it real.

@solution
    2j then, four times: ^ t } i , reason <Esc> j

By the third pass you should no longer be checking where the cursor landed
before you press `i`. That is the point at which `t` has stopped being a
thing you think about and become a thing you aim with.

One habit this drill builds quietly: `u` after a line that went wrong, then
that line again, rather than nudging the text back into shape with more
insert-mode typing. Undoing and re-aiming is faster, and it trains the aim
instead of training the repair.

@start order.events.ts
export class OrderEvents {
  public async publish(orderId: string, reason: string) {
    this.events.emit('order.cancelled', { orderId });
    this.events.emit('order.refunded', { orderId });
    this.events.emit('order.reopened', { orderId });
    this.events.emit('order.settled', { orderId });
  }
}

@expected order.events.ts
export class OrderEvents {
  public async publish(orderId: string, reason: string) {
    this.events.emit('order.cancelled', { orderId, reason });
    this.events.emit('order.refunded', { orderId, reason });
    this.events.emit('order.reopened', { orderId, reason });
    this.events.emit('order.settled', { orderId, reason });
  }
}
